A small min-heap is enough here. It holds the largest `k` values seen so far. When a new number makes the heap too large, the smallest value is removed. That discarded value cannot be in the final top `k`.

Dry run for `nums = [3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4]` and `k = 2`:

- Keep `3, 2`, then remove `1`.
- Add `5`, so `2` is removed.
- Add `6`, so `3` is removed.
- `4` is smaller than the heap minimum `5`, so it is removed instead.

The heap ends with `5` and `6`, and its top is `5`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <functional>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int kthLargestElement(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minHeap;

        for (int value : nums) {
            minHeap.push(value);
            if (static_cast<int>(minHeap.size()) > k) {
                minHeap.pop();
            }
        }

        return minHeap.top();
    }
};
```

The input array is not changed. This is especially useful when `k` is much smaller than `n`, since the heap never grows beyond `k` elements.

Time complexity: `O(n log k)`

Space complexity: `O(k)`.

QuickSelect places one pivot in its final sorted position, then continues only on the side that contains the target. For the `k`th largest value, the zero-based target index is `nums.length - k`.

The pivot is chosen randomly to reduce the chance of repeatedly getting a poor split. This code rearranges the input array.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

class Solution {
    public int kthLargestElement(int[] nums, int k) {
        int target = nums.length - k;
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int pivotIndex = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(left, right + 1);
            int finalIndex = partition(nums, left, right, pivotIndex);

            if (finalIndex == target) {
                return nums[finalIndex];
            }
            if (finalIndex < target) {
                left = finalIndex + 1;
            } else {
                right = finalIndex - 1;
            }
        }

        return nums[target];
    }

    private int partition(int[] nums, int left, int right, int pivotIndex) {
        int pivot = nums[pivotIndex];
        swap(nums, pivotIndex, right);

        int store = left;
        for (int i = left; i < right; i++) {
            if (nums[i] < pivot) {
                swap(nums, i, store);
                store++;
            }
        }

        swap(nums, store, right);
        return store;
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int first, int second) {
        int temp = nums[first];
        nums[first] = nums[second];
        nums[second] = temp;
    }
}
```

For `k = 1`, the target is the last index. For `k = nums.length`, it is the first index. The average running time is good, but a bad series of pivots can still lead to quadratic time.

Time complexity: `O(n)` average, `O(n^2)` worst case

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This version searches possible answer values instead of rearranging the array. For a trial value `middle`, it counts how many numbers are at least `middle`.

If that count is at least `k`, `middle` could still be the answer, so the search moves higher. Otherwise it moves lower.

A small dry run with `[3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4]` and `k = 2`: values `>= 5` occur twice, so `5` is possible. Values `>= 6` occur once, so the answer cannot be `6`. The largest valid trial value is `5`.

```go
func KthLargestElement(nums []int, k int) int {
    low, high := nums[0], nums[0]

    for _, value := range nums[1:] {
        if value < low {
            low = value
        }
        if value > high {
            high = value
        }
    }

    for low < high {
        middle := low + (high-low+1)/2
        count := 0

        for _, value := range nums {
            if value >= middle {
                count++
            }
        }

        if count >= k {
            low = middle
        } else {
            high = middle - 1
        }
    }

    return low
}
```

The array stays unchanged. This also handles negative values because the search limits come directly from the input. With one element, `low` and `high` are already equal.

Time complexity: `O(n log R)`, where `R` is the input value range

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A max-heap gives the largest remaining number first. I add every array value to it, remove the largest `k - 1` times, and then read the next value.

This keeps the code close to the meaning of the problem. It stores all values, so it is not as space-friendly as a heap limited to size `k`.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int kthLargestElement(int[] nums, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b, a)
        );

        for (int value : nums) {
            maxHeap.offer(value);
        }

        for (int removed = 1; removed < k; removed++) {
            maxHeap.poll();
        }

        return maxHeap.peek();
    }
}
```

An array with one value is handled naturally because the loop does not remove anything. Duplicate values are counted as separate positions.

Time complexity: `O(n + k log n)`

Space complexity: `O(n)`.

