## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int kthLargestElement(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int pivot = 0;

        int left = 0;
        int right = n - 1;

        while (true) {
            pivot = find(nums, left, right);

            if (pivot < k - 1) {
                left = pivot + 1;
            } else if (pivot > k - 1) {
                right = pivot - 1;
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }

        return nums[pivot];
    }

    private int find(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        int id = left - 1;

        for (int j = left; j < right; j++) {
            if (nums[j] >= nums[right]) {
                id++;
                swap(nums, id, j);
            }
        }

        id++;
        swap(nums, id, right);
        return id;
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int x, int y) {
        int temp = nums[x];
        nums[x] = nums[y];
        nums[y] = temp;
    }
}

## Intuition

To find the K-th largest element, we can sort the array in descending order.

After sorting, the largest element will be at index 0, the second largest at index 1, and so on.

Therefore, the K-th largest element will be present at index `k-1`.

## Approach

1. Sort the array in descending order using `greater<int>()`.
2. After sorting, the K-th largest element will be at index `k-1`.
3. Return `nums[k-1]`.

For example:

`nums = [11, 9, 8, 7, 3, 1]` and `k = 4`

After sorting:

`[11, 9, 8, 7, 3, 1]`

The 4th largest element is `7`.

## Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int kthLargestElement(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end(), greater<int>());

        return nums[k - 1];
    }
};

